   "project": "fieldnotes",
   "reply": "`delete INDEX` is implemented, and all 6 tests and `tools/check.py` pass (exit 0).\n\n**Red step.** Before implementing, 3 of the 5 new tests failed:\n- Both `store.delete` tests raised `AttributeError: module 'fieldnotes.store' has no attribute 'delete'`.\n- `test_delete_removes_note` failed with `AssertionError: 2 != 0`, because the CLI returned its usage error.\n- `test_delete_out_of_range_fails` failed with `2 != 1`, for the same reason.\n- The non-integer test already passed, since an unknown command exits 2. It stays as a guard on the new argument parsing.\n\n**Implementation**\n- `fieldnotes/store.py`: new `delete(index)` removes and returns the note at a 1-based index. It raises `IndexError` if the index is out of range, and the file is left unchanged in that case. I pulled the file write into a small `_save` helper that `add` now uses too.\n- `fieldnotes/cli.py`: `delete INDEX` prints `deleted: <text>` and exits 0. An out-of-range index prints `no note at index N` to stderr and exits 1. A non-integer index gets the usage message and exit 2. I moved the usage string into a `USAGE` constant that now mentions `delete`.\n\n**Tests.** The store tests are in `tests/test_store.py`. The CLI tests are in the new `tests/test_cli.py`.\n\n`list` doesn't print indices, so you have to count lines to know which number to pass to `delete`. Having `list` show numbers would be an easy follow-up, but I left it alone because it changes existing output.",
